Having looked at all our suggestions, I would like to suggest the following as I prefer to have the requirement noted before what it prevents:

5.2.11 | [ADDED] Verify that the application appropriately sanitizes untrusted input before use in Java Naming and Directory Interface (JNDI) queries to prevent JNDI injection attacks. | ✓ | ✓ | ✓
-- | -- | -- | -- | --

@ImanSharaf I don't want to use the word "validates" as that sounds like input validation which shouldn't be a JNDI specific control. Jim also used the word sanitization which I think is appropriate unless you specifically think encoding is the correct term.

@jmanico @elarlang any final thoughts?

Here is an example in Java where validation really is the right defense.

![Screenshot 2023-09-26 at 1 02 23 PM](https://github.com/OWASP/ASVS/assets/531465/8b874225-4dd6-41a5-90fc-af36f1c0edf7)

You can also limit what JNDI can do:

```
Hashtable<String, String> env = new Hashtable<>();
env.put(Context.INITIAL_CONTEXT_FACTORY, "com.sun.jndi.rmi.registry.RegistryContextFactory");
env.put(Context.PROVIDER_URL, "rmi://localhost:1099");
InitialContext context = new InitialContext(env);
```

By setting the INITIAL_CONTEXT_FACTORY and PROVIDER_URL, you're limiting the types of services that the JNDI context can interact with.
